  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 325 AM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nRidging aloft that is building across the western U.S. today \nwill begin moving east into the central Plains on Thursday and\nFriday. A few additional shortwaves will eject off a parent low\nsitting and spinning over Quebec over the next few days, sending\na reinforcing burst of cold air behind today's front. The\nridging that builds into the area is expected to keep the bulk \nof the shortwave energy to our east, and the air should be dry \nenough to prevent any widespread precipitation even if lift does\nimpinge on our area, so kept PoPs at 0% for Thursday in our\neastern CWA. \n\nTemperatures for the rest of the work week will be cooler than \nthe first half of the week, with highs Thursday in the mid- to \nupper 50s near the Arkansas/Oklahoma state borders and low 50s \nnorthward. Lows Thursday night into Friday drop back below \nfreezing, ranging from 27-32 across most of the area. Friday's \ndaytime temperatures will range from mid-40s in central Missouri\nto low 50s furthest south, dropping into the low 30s in western\nMissouri/eastern Kansas and to the low to mid-20s in the \neastern Ozarks Friday night.\n\nHeight rises accompanying the ridging aloft may raise\ntemperatures into the mid- to upper 50s in portions of the area\nthis weekend, especially in far western Missouri and eastern\nKansas. However, as the next system looks to develop in the\nsouthern Plains on Sunday and Monday, these far west reaches \nhave a far larger temperature spread for the weekend due to \nuncertainty in extent and intensity of southerly flow and WAA \nassociated with this next potential system. Therefore, confidence\nthat these warmer temperatures will occur remains low, and most\noutcomes suggest temperatures in the low 50s during the day and\novernight lows in the upper 20s to low 30s each day this\nweekend.\n\nThis weekend system brings 15-45% PoPs to the area Sunday into\nMonday, with highest PoPs seemingly on Sunday evening in the far\nwestern extent of our CWA. This is also the area expected to\nhave the warmest temperatures, remaining 37-45 degrees during\nthe time period of highest PoPs, so the season's first snow\nflurries remain unlikely, with overall QPF values also low for\nareas that even see rainfall. Many areas, especially east of \nHighway 65, are most likely to remain completely dry Sunday and \nMonday with this system's passage. Stay tuned as the weekend \napproaches for more fine-tuned details.",
